"case": "An archivist must say which words Jane Austen uses most in her opening page, and whether 'truth' or 'fortune' comes up more often, without reading it twice.",
"hook": "Picture an index-card box. Every time you read a word, you find its card and add a tally mark; a new word gets a new card. At the end, the box is a word-frequency index. Python calls that box a dictionary.",
"concept": "A dictionary keeps pairs: a key (the word) and a value (its count). counts[word] looks up a card. counts.get(word, 0) looks it up and says 0 if there is no card yet. Sorting the pairs by value gives the top words.",
"term": "Dictionary",
"term_plain": "a lookup from a key to a value, like a word to its count",
"anim": "cards",
"worked": {
"title": "Worked example: count the words of one line",
"code": "line = \"the eye of heaven shines and the gold of the sun\"\ncounts = {}\nfor word in line.split():\n    counts[word] = counts.get(word, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)\nprint(counts[\"the\"])",
"expected": "{'the': 3, 'eye': 1, 'of': 2, 'heaven': 1, 'shines': 1, 'and': 1, 'gold': 1, 'sun': 1}\n3\n"
},
